654:{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Cross-backbone, task and horizon generalization.}\label{sec:cross_backbone}\label{sec:topk} RR-MoA wins all 21 MOMENT-small/large configurations ($26$--$79\%$; Table~\ref{tab:cross_backbone}), 18/18 Moirai ($3$--$31\%$), and 6/6 Moirai-MoE~\citep{liu2025moiraimoe} datasets ($37$--$90\%$; Table~\ref{tab:moirai_moe}): adapter-level and backbone-internal routing operate on complementary axes. The negative controls predicted by Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} (Chronos and Timer-XL~\citep{liu2025timerxl}, neither using instance normalization) show no collapse and no RR-MoA gain (4/18 within chance; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_backbone}). Top-2 sparse routing retains most of the dense gain at a $+10\%$ latency cost (Appendix~\ref{app:topk}); imputation ($7/8$; Appendix~\ref{app:imputation}) and horizons $H{\in}\{96{,}192{,}336{,}720\}$ (24/24; Table~\ref{tab:horizon}) confirm robustness. \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}} (Appendix~\ref{app:gate_pathology}) extends to all 6 datasets across 5 backbones and 4 horizons (Table~\ref{tab:residual_ia_plus_mhcb}).}
